1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,880
Listen.

2
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,040
Practice.

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:09,900
Speak.

4
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,370
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:14,810
Hello, friends.

6
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:18,400
Welcome back to English Listening Practice Podcast.

7
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:19,380
I'm Ryan.

8
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:20,340
And I'm Chloe.

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,140
We're so happy you're spending your time learning English with us today.

10
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,130
Today, we have a very useful and practical topic for you.

11
00:00:28,730 --> 00:00:31,590
Business meeting conversations at the office.

12
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:32,450
That's right.

13
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,220
Meetings are a huge part of office life.

14
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:44,420
Whether you work in sales, marketing, customer service, finance, or management, you'll probably attend many meetings during your career.

15
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:49,860
And sometimes meetings can feel stressful, especially if English is not your first language.

16
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,090
Exactly.

17
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:59,960
You may need to express ideas, ask questions, give updates, explain problems, or share opinions professionally.

18
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:07,070
That's why today's podcast will help you learn natural English conversations people use during business meetings every day.

19
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:14,670
We'll practice real office English with practical examples and clear explanations through natural conversations.

20
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:20,810
So relax, listen carefully and let's begin today's business meeting conversation podcast.

21
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:21,950
Good morning, everyone.

22
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:23,940
Thanks for joining today's meeting.

23
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:25,060
Good morning, Ryan.

24
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:26,140
Morning, everyone.

25
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:27,320
Shall we get started?

26
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:28,760
Yes, let's begin.

27
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:35,610
Today, we need to discuss the progress of the marketing campaign and review next month's sales targets.

28
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:36,630
Sounds good.

29
00:01:37,170 --> 00:01:40,690
Before we start, does everyone have a copy of the meeting agenda?

30
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:42,590
Yes, I received it this morning.

31
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:43,520
Same here.

32
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:44,200
Great.

33
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:47,680
That will help us stay organized and manage our time properly.

34
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:48,650
Exactly.

35
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:53,170
Meetings become much more productive when everyone understands the schedule.

36
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:54,200
All right, Ryan.

37
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,180
Would you like to begin with the sales update?

38
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:56,900
Sure.

39
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:03,700
Overall, sales increased by 12% this month, which is definitely positive news for the company.

40
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:05,390
That's excellent progress.

41
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:10,130
Yes, especially considering how competitive the market has become recently.

42
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:12,690
What do you think helped increase sales this month?

43
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:17,820
I believe our social media advertising campaign played a major role.

44
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:18,780
That makes sense.

45
00:02:19,230 --> 00:02:22,350
We received much more online engagement compared to previous months.

46
00:02:22,890 --> 00:02:23,470
Exactly.

47
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:27,780
Customers responded very positively to the short promotional videos.

48
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:29,140
I noticed that too.

49
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,940
The video posts received significantly more comments and shares.

50
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:33,710
Right.

51
00:02:34,110 --> 00:02:37,730
That kind of interaction helps improve brand visibility.

52
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:38,500
Absolutely.

53
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:43,570
More visibility usually leads to more customer interest and eventually more sales.

54
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:44,730
Exactly.

55
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:49,800
That's why digital marketing has become such an important investment for companies.

56
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:51,280
I completely agree.

57
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:55,120
However, we also faced a few challenges this month.

58
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:56,610
What kind of challenges?

59
00:02:57,130 --> 00:03:01,890
Some customers complained about delayed deliveries during the second week of the campaign.

60
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,380
Oh yes, I heard about that issue.

61
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:10,730
Unfortunately, the logistics department experienced unexpected delays because of supplier problems.

62
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:13,050
That must have been frustrating for customers.

63
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:14,560
It definitely was.

64
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,680
A few clients contacted customer service several times asking for updates.

65
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:22,120
Did we manage to resolve the problem quickly?

66
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:23,510
Thankfully, yes.

67
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:29,240
The logistics team worked overtime to fix the delays before the situation became worse.

68
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:30,620
That's good to hear.

69
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:36,930
Still, situations like this remind us how important communication is during business operations.

70
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:38,030
Absolutely.

71
00:03:38,490 --> 00:03:43,180
Customers become much more understanding when companies communicate honestly about problems.

72
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:44,280
Exactly.

73
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:50,060
Even when mistakes happen, professional communication can protect customer relationships.

74
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:51,910
That's a very important point.

75
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:55,450
All right, let's move on to next month's marketing plans.

76
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:56,230
Sure.

77
00:03:56,270 --> 00:04:00,330
I prepared a few ideas for increasing online engagement further.

78
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:00,760
Great.

79
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:02,650
We'd love to hear your suggestions.

80
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:07,000
First, I think we should create more behind-the-scenes content for social media.

81
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:08,360
Interesting idea.

82
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:10,060
Why do you think that would help?

83
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,270
Customers enjoy seeing the human side of companies.

84
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:17,180
It helps build trust and stronger emotional connections with the brand.

85
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:17,990
That's true.

86
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:23,790
People usually connect more with authentic content than overly polished advertisements.

87
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:24,810
Exactly.

88
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:32,330
For example, short videos showing employees working together or preparing products often receive strong engagement online.

89
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:33,790
That makes a lot of sense.

90
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:39,480
I also think we should improve communication with customers through live question and answer sessions online.

91
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:41,680
That's another excellent suggestion.

92
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:47,130
Live sessions create direct interaction and help customers feel more connected to the company.

93
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:48,470
I agree completely.

94
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:54,860
Plus, they allow us to answer customer concerns immediately instead of waiting for email responses.

95
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:55,860
Very true.

96
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,420
Faster communication usually improves customer satisfaction.

97
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:00,500
Exactly.

98
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:04,500
Does anyone else have ideas or feedback regarding the marketing strategy?

99
00:05:04,930 --> 00:05:06,370
I'd like to add something.

100
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:06,770
Of course.

101
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:07,370
Go ahead.

102
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:13,880
I think we should also focus more on customer retention instead of only attracting new customers.

103
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:15,200
That's an important point.

104
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:16,520
Could you explain further?

105
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:17,330
Sure.

106
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:25,360
Acquiring new customers is important, but maintaining strong relationships with existing customers is equally valuable.

107
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:26,460
Absolutely.

108
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:30,490
Loyal customers often generate consistent long-term revenue.

109
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:31,550
Exactly.

110
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:36,620
We could introduce loyalty rewards or special discounts for returning customers.

111
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:38,480
I really like that idea.

112
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:44,190
Small rewards can encourage repeat purchases and improve customer loyalty significantly.

113
00:05:44,230 --> 00:05:48,900
I think that strategy would work very well, especially for our online customers.

114
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:49,840
I agree.

115
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:54,050
All right, let's discuss the budget for next month's campaigns.

116
00:05:54,490 --> 00:05:57,650
Honestly, this might be the most difficult part of today's meeting.

117
00:05:57,970 --> 00:05:58,870
Probably true.

118
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,330
Budget discussions are never easy.

119
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,720
Especially when every department wants additional funding.

120
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:05,760
Exactly.

121
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:10,840
Unfortunately, we need to prioritize carefully because resources are limited.

122
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:13,870
Do we have an estimated budget increase for next quarter?

123
00:06:14,370 --> 00:06:18,960
Management approved a small increase, but not as much as we originally requested.

124
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:20,350
That's disappointing.

125
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:21,540
A little, yes.

126
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,210
However, we still have enough resources to continue our major campaigns.

127
00:06:26,670 --> 00:06:27,830
That's reassuring at least.

128
00:06:28,310 --> 00:06:28,810
Exactly.

129
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:33,880
We simply need to spend more strategically and avoid unnecessary expenses.

130
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:35,020
That makes sense.

131
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:42,010
Instead of investing heavily in traditional advertising, we might achieve better results through digital campaigns.

132
00:06:42,510 --> 00:06:43,450
I completely agree.

133
00:06:43,870 --> 00:06:48,140
Digital marketing often provides stronger measurable results for lower costs.

134
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:48,990
Exactly.

135
00:06:49,410 --> 00:06:54,620
Plus, online campaigns allow us to track customer behavior much more accurately.

136
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:58,370
That data is extremely valuable for future business decisions.

137
00:06:58,950 --> 00:06:59,630
Absolutely.

138
00:07:00,110 --> 00:07:01,550
Ryan, may I ask something?

139
00:07:01,970 --> 00:07:02,390
Of course.

140
00:07:02,830 --> 00:07:05,260
Are we planning to hire additional staff next quarter?

141
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:08,360
Management is currently discussing that possibility.

142
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:09,220
I see.

143
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:14,110
Honestly, several teams are already handling very heavy workloads.

144
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:15,280
I've noticed that too.

145
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:18,060
Many employees have been working overtime recently.

146
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:19,120
Exactly.

147
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:25,170
And while everyone appreciates the team's hard work, long-term burnout is a serious concern.

148
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:26,190
Definitely.

149
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:29,120
Employee well-being should always remain important.

150
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:30,680
I completely agree.

151
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:35,200
Productivity usually decreases when employees become exhausted.

152
00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:36,230
That's very true.

153
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:39,220
Rested employees generally perform much better.

154
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:40,280
Exactly.

155
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:44,450
That's why management has been discussing better workload distribution.

156
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:48,600
Hopefully new hiring decisions will help reduce pressure on the current teams.

157
00:07:48,830 --> 00:07:49,640
That's the goal.

158
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:53,380
All right, should we move on to the client feedback section?

159
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:54,800
Yes, let's continue.

160
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:58,910
Overall, customer feedback has been mostly positive this month.

161
00:07:59,390 --> 00:08:00,270
That's excellent news.

162
00:08:00,710 --> 00:08:05,120
Customers especially praised the improved response times from customer support.

163
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:10,610
That's great to hear because the support team worked very hard improving communication processes recently.

164
00:08:11,070 --> 00:08:12,110
It definitely shows.

165
00:08:12,590 --> 00:08:14,610
Were there any major complaints?

166
00:08:14,630 --> 00:08:18,660
A few customers mentioned difficulties navigating the website during checkout.

167
00:08:18,790 --> 00:08:19,650
Ah, yes.

168
00:08:19,830 --> 00:08:20,950
I heard about that issue.

169
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:25,380
Apparently, some users experienced technical problems while making payments.

170
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:28,360
That's definitely something we need to fix quickly.

171
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:29,220
I agree.

172
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:33,830
Even small technical problems can negatively impact customer trust.

173
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:34,450
Exactly.

174
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:39,290
Customers expect online services to function smoothly and efficiently.

175
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:43,580
Especially nowadays when people have many alternatives available online.

176
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:44,640
Very true.

177
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,410
Do we know what caused the website issue?

178
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:50,310
The IT department is still investigating the problem.

179
00:08:50,690 --> 00:08:53,940
Hopefully they can solve it before the next campaign begins.

180
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:55,120
That's the plan.

181
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:57,380
Ryan, can I share another suggestion?

182
00:08:57,860 --> 00:08:58,420
Absolutely.

183
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:02,750
I think we should improve communication between departments during major projects.

184
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:03,800
Interesting point.

185
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:05,320
Why do you feel that way?

186
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:09,890
Sometimes different departments work separately without sharing updates regularly.

187
00:09:10,270 --> 00:09:12,070
That can create confusion and delays.

188
00:09:12,570 --> 00:09:14,450
I completely understand what you mean.

189
00:09:14,510 --> 00:09:22,440
For example, last month, the sales team launched a promotion before customer support received all the updated information.

190
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:25,140
Ah, yes, I remember that situation.

191
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:31,290
Customers started asking questions immediately, but some support agents didn't know about the promotion yet.

192
00:09:31,330 --> 00:09:34,280
That definitely created unnecessary confusion.

193
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:35,300
Exactly.

194
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:39,960
Better communication between departments could prevent similar situations in the future.

195
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:41,450
I agree completely.

196
00:09:42,130 --> 00:09:46,030
Maybe we should organize shorter weekly update meetings between departments.

197
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:48,170
That's actually an excellent idea.

198
00:09:48,230 --> 00:09:52,040
Even quick 15-minute meetings could improve teamwork a lot.

199
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:53,000
I agree.

200
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:54,380
All right, everyone.

201
00:09:54,580 --> 00:09:58,400
Before we finish, let's quickly review today's action items.

202
00:09:58,570 --> 00:09:59,130
Good idea.

203
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:02,320
That will help everyone stay clear about responsibilities.

204
00:10:02,470 --> 00:10:08,710
First, the marketing team will continue focusing on video content and customer engagement strategies.

205
00:10:09,170 --> 00:10:09,500
Got it.

206
00:10:09,890 --> 00:10:14,780
Second, the IT department will prioritize fixing the website checkout issue.

207
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:15,620
Understood.

208
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:21,310
Third, management will continue reviewing staffing needs and workload distribution.

209
00:10:21,730 --> 00:10:22,250
Sounds good.

210
00:10:22,630 --> 00:10:29,150
And finally, we'll begin organizing shorter cross-department communication meetings starting next week.

211
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:31,180
I think that will improve teamwork a lot.

212
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:31,820
I agree.

213
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:35,310
Honestly, today's meeting felt very productive.

214
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:36,500
I think so too.

215
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:40,290
Everyone shared useful ideas and constructive feedback.

216
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:42,300
That's what makes meetings valuable.

217
00:10:42,650 --> 00:10:45,050
Collaboration and clear communication.

218
00:10:45,550 --> 00:10:46,050
Exactly.

219
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:50,510
Meetings should help teams solve problems together, not simply waste time.

220
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:51,640
Very true.

221
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:56,240
Does anyone have final questions or comments before we end today's meeting?

222
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:57,240
Nothing for me.

223
00:10:57,660 --> 00:10:58,110
Same here.

224
00:10:58,490 --> 00:10:58,990
All right then.

225
00:10:59,010 --> 00:11:02,310
Thank you everyone for your time and hard work today.

226
00:11:02,290 --> 00:11:03,310
Thank you, everyone.

227
00:11:03,590 --> 00:11:04,760
Have a productive afternoon.

228
00:11:05,220 --> 00:11:06,060
See you all tomorrow.

229
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:07,160
All right, everyone.

230
00:11:07,340 --> 00:11:12,760
We've reached the end of today's... Hello, everyone, and welcome back to Business English Corner.

231
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:13,580
I'm Ethan.

232
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:14,540
And I'm Mia.

233
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:18,390
We're very happy you're spending time learning English with us today.

234
00:11:18,370 --> 00:11:26,540
If you work an office, attend meetings, or want to sound more professional in business situations, today's lesson is perfect for you.

235
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:27,420
That's right.

236
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:32,250
Today, we're going to practice real business meeting conversations at the office.

237
00:11:32,710 --> 00:11:44,330
We'll talk about team meetings, project discussions, deadlines, presentations, problem solving, negotiations, updates, disagreements, and professional communication.

238
00:11:44,310 --> 00:11:51,140
And we'll explain why people say certain things during meetings, so you can understand the meaning behind the language, too.

239
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:52,240
Exactly.

240
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:56,530
In real business meetings, people don't only exchange information.

241
00:11:56,890 --> 00:12:01,430
They also try to sound professional, polite, confident, and cooperative.

242
00:12:01,830 --> 00:12:08,120
That's why learning business English is not only about vocabulary, it's also about communication style.

243
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:08,880
Very true.

244
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:12,310
All right, Mia, are you ready for today's office meetings?

245
00:12:12,770 --> 00:12:13,830
As ready as I can be.

246
00:12:14,270 --> 00:12:15,190
Let's get started.

247
00:12:15,550 --> 00:12:16,290
Good morning, Mia.

248
00:12:16,590 --> 00:12:17,240
Morning, Ethan.

249
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:18,800
You look busy already.

250
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:20,380
I definitely am.

251
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:22,220
We have three meetings today.

252
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:22,900
Three?

253
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:24,500
That sounds exhausting.

254
00:12:24,990 --> 00:12:26,090
It probably will be.

255
00:12:26,470 --> 00:12:27,750
What's the first meeting about?

256
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:29,850
The quarterly sales review.

257
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:30,650
Oh, right.

258
00:12:30,970 --> 00:12:34,500
The management team wanted updated numbers from every department.

259
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:35,340
Exactly.

260
00:12:35,700 --> 00:12:36,880
Did you finish your report?

261
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:38,120
Late last night.

262
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:39,400
Sounds stressful.

263
00:12:39,830 --> 00:12:42,510
It was, but at least everything is ready now.

264
00:12:42,890 --> 00:12:44,030
That's the important part.

265
00:12:44,330 --> 00:12:44,930
What about you?

266
00:12:45,270 --> 00:12:46,970
Are you prepared for your presentation?

267
00:12:47,390 --> 00:12:47,860
Mostly.

268
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,360
I still want to practice a few sections before the meeting starts.

269
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:52,220
Nervous?

270
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:53,000
A little.

271
00:12:53,340 --> 00:12:55,090
That's normal before presentations.

272
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:59,810
I know, but presenting in front of senior management always makes me anxious.

273
00:12:59,790 --> 00:13:02,560
Honestly, everyone feels that way sometimes.

274
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:03,440
Even you?

275
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:04,520
Absolutely.

276
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:05,700
That's surprising.

277
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:10,350
Confidence usually comes from preparation, not from never feeling nervous.

278
00:13:10,730 --> 00:13:11,990
That actually makes sense.

279
00:13:12,430 --> 00:13:15,200
The more prepared you are, the more confident you sound.

280
00:13:15,620 --> 00:13:18,000
I guess I should review my notes one more time then.

281
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:19,170
Good idea.

282
00:13:19,150 --> 00:13:21,030
Hey, Ethan, can I ask you something?

283
00:13:21,490 --> 00:13:21,710
Sure.

284
00:13:22,090 --> 00:13:25,010
During meetings, you always sound professional and calm.

285
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:25,910
How do you do that?

286
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:29,380
Honestly, I focus on speaking clearly and simply.

287
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:30,080
Really?

288
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:30,760
Yeah.

289
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:35,020
Many people think professional English means using complicated words.

290
00:13:35,450 --> 00:13:36,430
But that's not true?

291
00:13:36,890 --> 00:13:37,810
Not necessarily.

292
00:13:38,290 --> 00:13:41,810
Good business communication is usually clear and direct.

293
00:13:41,790 --> 00:13:42,870
That's helpful to know.

294
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:46,740
Also, I try to organize my ideas before speaking.

295
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:48,940
So you don't just speak immediately?

296
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:50,120
Not usually.

297
00:13:50,610 --> 00:13:53,010
I quickly think about my main point first.

298
00:13:53,450 --> 00:13:54,050
That's smart.

299
00:13:54,430 --> 00:13:56,910
It helps avoid confusion during discussions.

300
00:13:57,370 --> 00:14:00,640
I sometimes start talking before I fully organize my thoughts.

301
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:03,100
A lot of people do that during meetings.

302
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:07,730
Then halfway through my explanation, I realize I'm not being clear.

303
00:14:08,230 --> 00:14:10,650
That's why short pauses are useful.

304
00:14:10,630 --> 00:14:11,290
Pauses?

305
00:14:11,750 --> 00:14:12,050
Yeah.

306
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:16,940
Taking a second to think actually makes you sound more confident, not less.

307
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:17,960
Interesting.

308
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:20,690
Rushing usually creates more mistakes.

309
00:14:21,130 --> 00:14:22,530
I'll try to remember that today.

310
00:14:22,930 --> 00:14:23,850
Good morning, everyone.

311
00:14:24,150 --> 00:14:25,160
Let's begin the meeting.

312
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:25,920
Morning.

313
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:29,600
Thank you all for joining today's quarterly review meeting.

314
00:14:29,750 --> 00:14:32,880
Before we begin, does everyone have the updated agenda?

315
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:33,480
Yes.

316
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:34,220
Great.

317
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:41,050
Today, we'll discuss sales performance, project progress, budgeting, and goals for next quarter.

318
00:14:41,470 --> 00:14:42,050
Sounds good.

319
00:14:42,490 --> 00:14:44,410
Let's start with the sales department.

320
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:46,700
Ethan, could you give us an update?

321
00:14:46,680 --> 00:14:47,220
Of course.

322
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:51,230
Overall, sales increased by 12% this quarter.

323
00:14:51,650 --> 00:14:52,130
Excellent.

324
00:14:52,570 --> 00:14:55,210
Most of the growth came from online customers.

325
00:14:55,630 --> 00:14:56,180
Interesting.

326
00:14:56,640 --> 00:15:01,410
Our digital marketing campaigns performed especially well during the last two months.

327
00:15:01,830 --> 00:15:02,710
That's great news.

328
00:15:03,150 --> 00:15:07,080
However, retail store performance remained mostly unchanged.

329
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:08,600
Do we know why?

330
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:12,970
We believe customer behavior is shifting more towards online shopping.

331
00:15:13,370 --> 00:15:14,510
That makes sense.

332
00:15:14,530 --> 00:15:19,170
We're currently analyzing additional customer data to better understand the trend.

333
00:15:19,550 --> 00:15:20,170
Good approach.

334
00:15:20,650 --> 00:15:24,040
We also noticed stronger engagement from younger customers.

335
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:24,800
Really?

336
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:25,520
Yes.

337
00:15:26,020 --> 00:15:31,150
Social media campaigns appear to be influencing purchasing decisions significantly.

338
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:32,470
Interesting observation.

339
00:15:32,910 --> 00:15:38,120
Because of that, we recommend increasing investment in digital advertising next quarter.

340
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:40,000
I agree with that recommendation.

341
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:40,780
Thank you.

342
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:45,680
Notice how Ethan explained both the positive results and the remaining challenges.

343
00:15:46,210 --> 00:15:46,450
Right.

344
00:15:46,930 --> 00:15:50,170
In business meetings, balanced communication is important.

345
00:15:50,610 --> 00:15:51,150
Exactly.

346
00:15:51,530 --> 00:15:55,600
If you only talk about positive results, it can sound unrealistic.

347
00:15:55,580 --> 00:15:59,700
And if you only focus on problems, it may sound too negative.

348
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:05,710
Professional communication usually includes progress, challenges, and possible solutions.

349
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:07,090
Very well said.

350
00:16:07,650 --> 00:16:08,970
All right, let's continue.

351
00:16:09,430 --> 00:16:12,250
Mia, could you update us on the marketing strategy?

352
00:16:12,690 --> 00:16:13,130
Certainly.

353
00:16:13,590 --> 00:16:18,740
Over the past three months, our marketing team focused on increasing customer engagement.

354
00:16:19,160 --> 00:16:19,540
Okay.

355
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:25,590
We launched several new social media campaigns and improved email marketing performance.

356
00:16:25,570 --> 00:16:27,430
Did the campaigns perform well?

357
00:16:27,830 --> 00:16:28,090
Yes.

358
00:16:28,590 --> 00:16:31,120
Engagement rates increased by nearly 20%.

359
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:32,520
Excellent.

360
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:36,890
Video content performed especially well among younger audiences.

361
00:16:37,310 --> 00:16:39,150
That's becoming more common these days.

362
00:16:39,650 --> 00:16:40,230
Exactly.

363
00:16:40,670 --> 00:16:43,220
Customer preferences are changing quickly.

364
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:44,580
Were there any difficulties?

365
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:45,440
A few.

366
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:46,400
Like what?

367
00:16:46,850 --> 00:16:49,390
Some campaigns exceeded the original budget.

368
00:16:49,790 --> 00:16:50,190
I see.

369
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:53,580
Advertising costs increased more than expected.

370
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:55,880
How are you planning to manage that issue?

371
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:59,490
We're reviewing campaign efficiency more carefully now.

372
00:16:59,910 --> 00:17:00,470
Good idea.

373
00:17:00,930 --> 00:17:05,220
We also plan to prioritize platforms that generate stronger results.

374
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:06,660
That sounds reasonable.

375
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:07,600
Thank you.

376
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:13,090
Notice how Mia explained the problem clearly, but also immediately offered solutions.

377
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:23,740
Complaining without offering ideas rarely sounds professional.

378
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:24,120
Right.

379
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:27,840
Even if there's a problem, people expect constructive thinking.

380
00:17:28,260 --> 00:17:30,090
That's a very important business skill.

381
00:17:30,550 --> 00:17:32,330
Excuse me, may I add something?

382
00:17:32,730 --> 00:17:33,130
Of course.

383
00:17:33,630 --> 00:17:37,920
I think we should also consider improving internal communication between departments.

384
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:39,220
Interesting point.

385
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:41,510
Why do you think that's necessary?

386
00:17:41,480 --> 00:17:45,350
Sometimes our messaging and sales strategies feel disconnected.

387
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:46,820
I see what you mean.

388
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:51,010
Better communication between departments could improve consistency.

389
00:17:51,430 --> 00:17:53,330
That's actually a very good suggestion.

390
00:17:53,770 --> 00:17:54,100
Thanks.

391
00:17:54,580 --> 00:17:56,540
What would you recommend specifically?

392
00:17:56,520 --> 00:18:00,050
Maybe weekly coordination meetings between both teams.

393
00:18:00,470 --> 00:18:01,690
That could definitely help.

394
00:18:02,170 --> 00:18:04,730
It would allow faster information sharing, too.

395
00:18:05,150 --> 00:18:05,550
Agreed.

396
00:18:05,610 --> 00:18:10,820
Notice how the discussion stayed respectful even while people offered different ideas.

397
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:13,660
That's very important in professional meetings.

398
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:16,290
Business discussions are not arguments.

399
00:18:16,590 --> 00:18:18,410
They're collaborative conversations.

400
00:18:18,930 --> 00:18:19,490
Exactly.

401
00:18:20,010 --> 00:18:23,340
Even disagreements should sound polite and constructive.

402
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:33,190
For example, instead of saying, that idea is wrong, professionals often say things like, I see your point, but… or, have we considered another option?

403
00:18:33,610 --> 00:18:36,000
Those phrases sound much more diplomatic.

404
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:39,640
And diplomacy matters a lot in workplace communication.

405
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:42,810
Especially in meetings with managers or clients.

406
00:18:43,310 --> 00:18:43,930
Absolutely.

407
00:18:44,390 --> 00:18:47,730
All right, everyone, let's move on to project deadlines.

408
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:48,530
Okay.

409
00:18:48,830 --> 00:18:53,860
Several projects are approaching final stages, so timing is extremely important.

410
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:54,780
Understood.

411
00:18:55,260 --> 00:18:58,550
Ethan, how's the software development project progressing?

412
00:18:59,030 --> 00:19:00,630
Overall, things are moving well.

413
00:19:01,050 --> 00:19:01,330
Good.

414
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:04,600
Most major features have already been completed.

415
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:05,460
Excellent.

416
00:19:05,860 --> 00:19:09,170
However, the testing phase is taking longer than expected.

417
00:19:09,610 --> 00:19:11,350
Is that going to affect the deadline?

418
00:19:11,710 --> 00:19:12,230
Possibly.

419
00:19:12,590 --> 00:19:13,900
Could you explain why?

420
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:18,220
The development team discovered several technical issues during system testing.

421
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:19,460
Serious issues?

422
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:23,450
Not critical, but they still require attention before launch.

423
00:19:23,910 --> 00:19:24,550
I understand.

424
00:19:25,030 --> 00:19:28,300
We don't want to release a product with unresolved problems.

425
00:19:28,700 --> 00:19:29,480
Definitely not.

426
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:31,660
That could damage customer trust.

427
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:32,680
Exactly.

428
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:36,770
Because of that, we may need an additional week before launch.

429
00:19:37,210 --> 00:19:38,510
I appreciate your honesty.

430
00:19:38,890 --> 00:19:39,290
Thank you.

431
00:19:39,750 --> 00:19:43,040
Sometimes people are afraid to mention delays in meetings.

432
00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:43,560
True.

433
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:47,480
But hiding problems usually create bigger problems later.

434
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:48,640
Exactly.

435
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:52,150
Professional communication requires transparency.

436
00:19:52,550 --> 00:19:55,470
Especially regarding deadlines and project risks.

437
00:19:55,970 --> 00:20:00,540
Managers generally prefer early warnings instead of last-minute surprises.

438
00:20:01,040 --> 00:20:01,660
Very true.

439
00:20:02,160 --> 00:20:03,720
Excuse me, I have a question.

440
00:20:04,080 --> 00:20:04,540
Go ahead.

441
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:09,550
If the project is delayed by one week, will that affect the marketing schedule too?

442
00:20:10,010 --> 00:20:11,510
Most likely, yes.

443
00:20:11,490 --> 00:20:14,950
That could create complications for our advertising campaign.

444
00:20:15,390 --> 00:20:16,600
I understand the concern.

445
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:20,040
We already scheduled several promotional activities.

446
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:24,530
Then we should probably coordinate closely before making a final decision.

447
00:20:25,010 --> 00:20:25,370
Agreed.

448
00:20:25,890 --> 00:20:30,880
Notice how the discussion stayed respectful even while people offered different ideas.

449
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:32,180
That's an important point.

450
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:37,330
In strong business environments, teamwork matters more than individual blame.

451
00:20:37,790 --> 00:20:38,370
Exactly.

452
00:20:38,770 --> 00:20:41,880
Productive meetings focus on progress and solutions.

453
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:44,040
Not on creating conflict.

454
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:47,430
Hey, Mia, can I ask something about meetings in general?

455
00:20:47,890 --> 00:20:48,170
Sure.

456
00:20:48,590 --> 00:20:51,860
What's the most difficult part of leading meetings for you?

457
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:54,300
Probably managing different personalities.

458
00:20:54,820 --> 00:20:55,380
What do you mean?

459
00:20:55,940 --> 00:20:59,410
Some people talk too much while others barely speak at all.

460
00:20:59,780 --> 00:21:00,360
That's true.

461
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:03,580
A good meeting leader needs to balance participation.

462
00:21:04,070 --> 00:21:06,370
So everyone gets a chance to contribute?

463
00:21:06,770 --> 00:21:07,350
Exactly.

464
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:09,120
That sounds challenging.

465
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:10,480
It definitely can be.

466
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:13,380
Especially when strong opinions are involved.

467
00:21:13,870 --> 00:21:14,510
Absolutely.

468
00:21:14,930 --> 00:21:17,790
Have you ever experienced a really difficult meeting?

469
00:21:18,230 --> 00:21:18,670
Oh yes.

470
00:21:19,210 --> 00:21:19,760
What happened?

471
00:21:20,180 --> 00:21:25,440
At my previous company, two department managers completely disagreed about a project budget.

472
00:21:25,790 --> 00:21:27,130
That sounds uncomfortable.

473
00:21:27,530 --> 00:21:28,930
Extremely uncomfortable.

474
00:21:29,310 --> 00:21:30,890
Did the meeting become emotional?

475
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:31,630
A little.

476
00:21:31,990 --> 00:21:33,080
That must have been awkward.

477
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:34,280
It was.

478
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:36,440
How was the situation resolved?

479
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:41,770
Eventually, the director stepped in and redirected the discussion towards facts and solutions.

480
00:21:42,270 --> 00:21:42,910
Smart move.

481
00:21:43,350 --> 00:21:46,450
Emotional discussions rarely help business decisions.

482
00:21:46,990 --> 00:21:47,700
Very true.

483
00:21:48,320 --> 00:21:51,680
That's why professionalism matters even during disagreements.

484
00:21:52,100 --> 00:21:53,800
Especially during disagreements.

485
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:54,920
Exactly.

486
00:21:55,530 --> 00:21:57,690
All right, everyone, let's continue the meeting.

487
00:21:58,170 --> 00:22:01,010
Next, we need to discuss budget planning for next quarter.

488
00:22:01,430 --> 00:22:01,950
Okay.

489
00:22:01,930 --> 00:22:12,300
Finance has recommended reducing op... Hello, everyone, and welcome back to the English at Work podcast, where we help you learn real, practical English used in business meetings at the office.

490
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:13,220
Hi, everyone.

491
00:22:13,260 --> 00:22:14,440
Great to have you here again.

492
00:22:14,480 --> 00:22:19,210
Today's episode is all about business meeting conversations at the office.

493
00:22:19,670 --> 00:22:23,080
We're going to show real meeting situations with natural English.

494
00:22:23,430 --> 00:22:29,280
Plus, we'll explain key phrases so you understand not just what people say, but why they say it.

495
00:22:29,720 --> 00:22:30,320
Exactly.

496
00:22:30,740 --> 00:22:32,350
This is not just listening practice.

497
00:22:32,630 --> 00:22:36,310
This is learning how office meetings actually sound in real companies.

498
00:22:36,460 --> 00:22:41,270
So imagine you've just joined a company and you're sitting in your first business meeting.

499
00:22:41,690 --> 00:22:42,130
Let's begin.

500
00:22:42,730 --> 00:22:43,550
Good morning, everyone.

501
00:22:43,970 --> 00:22:44,630
Let's get started.

502
00:22:45,070 --> 00:22:47,890
First of all, thank you for joining this weekly project meeting.

503
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:51,440
Before we begin, I just want to confirm the agenda.

504
00:22:51,860 --> 00:22:57,030
Today, we will cover project updates, client feedback, and next week's deadlines.

505
00:22:57,470 --> 00:22:58,150
Is that correct?

506
00:22:58,310 --> 00:22:59,380
Yes, that's correct.

507
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:00,280
Good question.

508
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:04,850
In business meetings, people often confirm the agenda so everyone is aligned.

509
00:23:05,370 --> 00:23:06,770
It helps avoid confusion later.

510
00:23:06,790 --> 00:23:07,670
That's right.

511
00:23:08,050 --> 00:23:14,160
When someone says, just to confirm, it means they want to double-check information to avoid mistakes.

512
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:14,780
All right.

513
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:16,760
Let's start with the project update.

514
00:23:17,340 --> 00:23:19,200
Sarah, could you give us a quick update?

515
00:23:19,660 --> 00:23:19,910
Sure.

516
00:23:19,930 --> 00:23:22,950
The development phase is 80% complete.

517
00:23:23,210 --> 00:23:28,800
We are currently testing the new features, but we've encountered a few technical issues with the login system.

518
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:32,970
When you say we've encountered issues, what does that mean exactly?

519
00:23:33,350 --> 00:23:33,930
Good question.

520
00:23:34,370 --> 00:23:38,710
Encountered issues is a polite business way of saying we found problems.

521
00:23:39,170 --> 00:23:42,360
It sounds more professional than just saying we have problems.

522
00:23:42,420 --> 00:23:43,030
Got it.

523
00:23:43,610 --> 00:23:45,450
So what's the impact of these issues?

524
00:23:46,070 --> 00:23:48,790
It may delay the release by two to three days.

525
00:23:49,190 --> 00:23:52,100
However, we are working to resolve it as quickly as possible.

526
00:23:52,620 --> 00:23:53,320
That's important.

527
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:59,620
In meetings, people often use phrases like as quickly as possible instead of giving exact timelines.

528
00:23:59,940 --> 00:24:01,640
It sounds more controlled and professional.

529
00:24:02,110 --> 00:24:02,590
Exactly.

530
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:05,530
Now, I'd like to highlight one important point.

531
00:24:05,910 --> 00:24:08,310
The client has requested additional features.

532
00:24:08,870 --> 00:24:12,410
Sorry to interrupt, but could you clarify what kind of features they want?

533
00:24:12,610 --> 00:24:16,620
Of course, they want a dashboard upgrade and better reporting tools.

534
00:24:17,160 --> 00:24:18,280
Thank you for clarifying.

535
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:21,460
Could you clarify is a very important meeting phrase.

536
00:24:21,980 --> 00:24:26,450
It means please explain more clearly, especially when something is not fully understood.

537
00:24:26,910 --> 00:24:29,610
Yes, and it's better than saying I don't understand.

538
00:24:30,050 --> 00:24:31,690
It sounds more professional and polite.

539
00:24:32,150 --> 00:24:34,450
Now let's move to client feedback discussion.

540
00:24:34,920 --> 00:24:37,120
We received feedback from the client yesterday.

541
00:24:37,580 --> 00:24:42,280
Overall, they are happy with the progress, but they mentioned concerns about loading speed.

542
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:47,310
When you say they're happy but, that structure is very common in business English, right?

543
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:48,030
Yes.

544
00:24:48,390 --> 00:24:50,130
It's used to show balance.

545
00:24:50,150 --> 00:24:53,230
You give positive feedback first, then mention the issue.

546
00:24:53,770 --> 00:24:55,620
So it softens the negative point.

547
00:24:56,080 --> 00:24:56,520
Exactly.

548
00:24:56,720 --> 00:24:58,760
It makes communication more diplomatic.

549
00:24:59,260 --> 00:25:01,420
What do we plan to do about the loading speed issue?

550
00:25:01,870 --> 00:25:04,150
The IT team is investigating the cause.

551
00:25:04,510 --> 00:25:06,950
We will prioritize optimization this week.

552
00:25:07,390 --> 00:25:11,260
Prioritize means something is more important than other tasks, correct?

553
00:25:11,640 --> 00:25:12,000
Yes.

554
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:16,290
It means we put that task at the top of our to-do list because it is urgent.

555
00:25:16,730 --> 00:25:17,010
Great.

556
00:25:17,430 --> 00:25:19,840
Now let's move to the next topic, deadlines.

557
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:22,540
We have a tight deadline for the next phase.

558
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:25,610
The final delivery is scheduled for next Friday.

559
00:25:26,050 --> 00:25:28,750
When someone says tight deadline, what does that mean?

560
00:25:28,810 --> 00:25:32,100
It means there is very little time to complete the work.

561
00:25:32,540 --> 00:25:34,840
It creates pressure because the timeline is short.

562
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:36,700
So how do we handle it?

563
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:42,450
We need to allocate resources efficiently and possibly extend working hours if necessary.

564
00:25:42,990 --> 00:25:45,400
Allocate resources sounds very formal.

565
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:46,460
Can you explain it?

566
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:47,060
Sure.

567
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:51,670
It means assigning people, time, and tools to different tasks.

568
00:25:51,690 --> 00:25:53,890
For example, deciding who works on what.

569
00:25:54,370 --> 00:25:55,030
That makes sense.

570
00:25:55,690 --> 00:25:57,480
Let's move to a discussion point.

571
00:25:57,980 --> 00:26:01,460
I'd like to bring up an issue regarding communication between teams.

572
00:26:02,070 --> 00:26:02,490
Go ahead.

573
00:26:02,850 --> 00:26:06,130
There seems to be a lack of clarity in task distribution.

574
00:26:06,550 --> 00:26:08,190
Some tasks are being duplicated.

575
00:26:08,730 --> 00:26:11,880
When you say bring up an issue, why do people use that phrase?

576
00:26:12,380 --> 00:26:16,680
It means introducing a problem into discussion in a formal and calm way.

577
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:21,070
Instead of saying we have a problem, we say I'd like to bring up an issue.

578
00:26:21,590 --> 00:26:23,030
That sounds more professional.

579
00:26:23,060 --> 00:26:23,580
Exactly.

580
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:28,630
Now, I suggest we improve coordination by using a shared task tracker.

581
00:26:29,030 --> 00:26:29,750
Good suggestion.

582
00:26:30,330 --> 00:26:36,080
In meetings, when someone says, I suggest, it introduces an idea politely instead of demanding change.

583
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:38,880
Yes, it keeps the conversation collaborative.

584
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:41,190
Now let's move to decision-making.

585
00:26:41,630 --> 00:26:44,630
So, what is the final decision regarding the timeline?

586
00:26:45,080 --> 00:26:49,300
After reviewing all points, we've decided to extend the deadline by three days.

587
00:26:49,550 --> 00:26:54,340
When you say, we've decided, it shows that a conclusion has been reached as a group, right?

588
00:26:54,780 --> 00:26:55,220
Exactly.

589
00:26:55,660 --> 00:26:57,160
It signals closure and agreement.

590
00:26:57,560 --> 00:26:57,860
All right.

591
00:26:58,040 --> 00:26:59,480
I'll update the team accordingly.

592
00:26:59,870 --> 00:27:00,290
Perfect.

593
00:27:00,890 --> 00:27:04,910
Now let's move to a more challenging situation, disagreement in a meeting.

594
00:27:05,350 --> 00:27:09,100
I understand your point, but I think we should consider another approach.

595
00:27:09,620 --> 00:27:10,200
What do you mean?

596
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:14,960
Instead of adding more features, we should focus on improving stability first.

597
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:16,830
That's an interesting perspective.

598
00:27:17,290 --> 00:27:19,070
However, I slightly disagree.

599
00:27:19,230 --> 00:27:22,960
When someone says, however, it shows polite disagreement, right?

600
00:27:23,340 --> 00:27:23,600
Yes.

601
00:27:24,040 --> 00:27:26,000
It helps you disagree without sounding rude.

602
00:27:26,440 --> 00:27:27,770
So, what's your concern?

603
00:27:28,310 --> 00:27:32,050
My concern is that reducing features might affect customer satisfaction.

604
00:27:32,550 --> 00:27:33,390
I see your point.

605
00:27:33,570 --> 00:27:34,620
That's a valid concern.

606
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:40,100
In meetings, saying, I see your point, is a polite way of showing respect even if you disagree.

607
00:27:40,120 --> 00:27:41,120
Exactly.

608
00:27:41,350 --> 00:27:43,170
It keeps the conversation professional.

609
00:27:43,650 --> 00:27:45,310
Maybe we can find a middle ground.

610
00:27:45,510 --> 00:27:49,860
Yes, perhaps we can launch a basic version first, then add features later.

611
00:27:50,300 --> 00:27:51,840
That sounds like a balanced solution.

612
00:27:52,180 --> 00:27:52,600
Great.

613
00:27:52,620 --> 00:27:53,980
Let's move forward with that idea.

614
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:56,440
Now let's talk about action items.

615
00:27:56,970 --> 00:27:59,970
Before we close the meeting, let's summarize action points.

616
00:28:00,550 --> 00:28:01,010
Good idea.

617
00:28:01,710 --> 00:28:05,160
Action points means tasks that need to be completed after the meeting.

618
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:06,080
Exactly.

619
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:10,090
So first action point, IT team will fix the login issue.

620
00:28:10,630 --> 00:28:14,030
Second action point, design team will review client feedback.

621
00:28:14,210 --> 00:28:17,440
Third action point, project manager will update the timeline.

622
00:28:17,840 --> 00:28:18,260
Perfect.

623
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:23,070
When someone summarizes like this, it ensures everyone is clear about responsibilities.

624
00:28:23,510 --> 00:28:25,230
Yes, it prevents confusion later.

625
00:28:25,750 --> 00:28:27,920
Now let's move to follow-up communication.

626
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:31,850
After meetings, we usually say, let's follow up on this, right?

627
00:28:32,230 --> 00:28:32,490
Yes.

628
00:28:33,010 --> 00:28:36,690
Follow-up means checking progress later or continuing the discussion.

629
00:28:36,930 --> 00:28:39,950
So I might say, I will follow up with you next week.

630
00:28:40,430 --> 00:28:40,870
Exactly.

631
00:28:41,130 --> 00:28:42,430
It keeps communication active.

632
00:28:42,890 --> 00:28:44,950
Now let's simulate a short client meeting.

633
00:28:45,390 --> 00:28:45,970
Good afternoon.

634
00:28:46,170 --> 00:28:47,180
Thank you for joining the call.

635
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:48,440
Thank you for having me.

636
00:28:48,680 --> 00:28:50,780
I'd like to get an update on the project status.

637
00:28:51,260 --> 00:28:54,820
We are currently on track, but we may need an extension for final testing.

638
00:28:55,240 --> 00:28:57,570
When you say on track, what does that mean?

639
00:28:57,590 --> 00:29:00,610
It means everything is progressing according to plan.

640
00:29:01,050 --> 00:29:01,550
Understood.

641
00:29:01,990 --> 00:29:04,350
Please keep us informed of any delays.

642
00:29:04,420 --> 00:29:04,880
Of course.

643
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:06,340
We will send weekly updates.

644
00:29:06,740 --> 00:29:07,740
That would be appreciated.

645
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:10,740
Now let's move to a closing meeting conversation.

646
00:29:11,220 --> 00:29:11,990
All right, everyone.

647
00:29:12,030 --> 00:29:14,070
I think that covers everything for today.

648
00:29:14,570 --> 00:29:16,310
Does anyone have any final questions?

649
00:29:16,830 --> 00:29:17,750
No questions for me.

650
00:29:18,170 --> 00:29:18,650
Great.

651
00:29:18,670 --> 00:29:19,410
Let's wrap this up.

652
00:29:19,840 --> 00:29:22,880
Let's wrap this up means let's finish the meeting, correct?

653
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:23,880
Exactly.

654
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:25,260
It's a common closing phrase.

655
00:29:25,660 --> 00:29:27,200
Thank you all for your input today.

656
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:28,450
Thank you, everyone.

657
00:29:28,630 --> 00:29:29,270
Great discussion.

658
00:29:29,750 --> 00:29:32,230
And that brings us to the end of today's episode.

659
00:29:32,390 --> 00:29:38,720
Today we practiced real business meeting conversations, including updates, disagreements, decisions, and action points.

660
00:29:39,180 --> 00:29:46,230
And we also explained key phrases like clarify, prioritize, bring up an issue, follow up, and many more.

661
00:29:46,710 --> 00:29:48,040
The key takeaway is this.

662
00:29:48,560 --> 00:29:51,120
Business English is not about complicated words.

663
00:29:51,540 --> 00:29:54,360
It's about clear, polite, and structured communication.

664
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:55,300
Exactly.

665
00:29:55,680 --> 00:30:01,810
The more you listen to these meeting conversations, the more confident you'll become in real office situations.

666
00:30:01,890 --> 00:30:04,220
Thanks for listening, and we'll see you in the next episode.

667
00:30:04,600 --> 00:30:07,680
Goodbye, and keep practicing your business English every day.

668
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:09,660
Hello, friends.

669
00:30:09,800 --> 00:30:12,850
Welcome back to English Listening Practice Podcast.

670
00:30:13,430 --> 00:30:14,010
I'm Jason.

671
00:30:14,450 --> 00:30:15,190
And I'm Mia.

672
00:30:15,350 --> 00:30:17,730
We're so happy you're learning English with us today.

673
00:30:18,220 --> 00:30:25,350
Today we have a very practical and exciting topic for you, business meeting conversations in 60 minutes.

674
00:30:25,830 --> 00:30:26,330
That's right.

675
00:30:26,810 --> 00:30:31,490
In today's episode, we're going to follow a full office meeting from beginning to end.

676
00:30:31,580 --> 00:30:43,870
You'll hear coworkers greeting each other, discussing business problems, sharing ideas, explaining decisions, handling disagreements professionally, planning future projects, and solving challenges together.

677
00:30:44,290 --> 00:30:51,400
We'll also explain why people say certain things in meetings and how professional communication works in real office situations.

678
00:30:51,960 --> 00:30:56,910
These are the kinds of conversations people use every day in companies around the world.

679
00:30:57,070 --> 00:31:04,850
So, whether you already work in an office or hope to work in one someday, this lesson will help you improve your business English naturally.

680
00:31:04,890 --> 00:31:06,130
All right, everyone.

681
00:31:06,370 --> 00:31:12,940
Grab your coffee, relax, and let's begin today's Business Meeting Conversations in 60 Minutes podcast.

682
00:31:13,540 --> 00:31:14,400
Good morning, everyone.

683
00:31:14,680 --> 00:31:17,270
Thanks for joining today's weekly strategy meeting.

684
00:31:17,710 --> 00:31:18,610
Good morning, Jason.

685
00:31:18,890 --> 00:31:19,650
Morning, everyone.

686
00:31:20,100 --> 00:31:20,880
Shall we get started?

687
00:31:21,360 --> 00:31:21,660
Yes.

688
00:31:22,020 --> 00:31:28,190
We have quite a few important topics to discuss today, so let's try to stay organized and manage our time carefully.

689
00:31:28,650 --> 00:31:29,150
Sounds good.

690
00:31:29,690 --> 00:31:34,760
First, I'd like to review last month's sales performance before we move on to new projects.

691
00:31:35,280 --> 00:31:35,600
All right.

692
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:37,800
I prepared the sales report this morning.

693
00:31:38,420 --> 00:31:38,840
Excellent.

694
00:31:39,100 --> 00:31:41,260
Could you share the main highlights with the team?

695
00:31:41,740 --> 00:31:42,140
Of course.

696
00:31:42,810 --> 00:31:48,390
Overall, sales increased by 8% compared to last month, which is definitely positive progress.

697
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:49,880
That's encouraging news.

698
00:31:50,400 --> 00:31:54,170
Yes, especially because the market has become much more competitive recently.

699
00:31:54,670 --> 00:31:55,330
Exactly.

700
00:31:55,610 --> 00:31:58,600
Many companies are lowering prices aggressively right now.

701
00:31:59,040 --> 00:31:59,660
That's true.

702
00:32:00,100 --> 00:32:04,100
However, our customer retention numbers remained strong despite the competition.

703
00:32:04,620 --> 00:32:06,350
That's actually very important.

704
00:32:06,770 --> 00:32:11,610
Loyal customers help companies maintain stability during difficult economic periods.

705
00:32:11,760 --> 00:32:12,440
Absolutely.

706
00:32:12,960 --> 00:32:18,900
Repeat customers usually spend more and require less advertising investment compared to attracting new customers.

707
00:32:19,460 --> 00:32:20,080
Very true.

708
00:32:20,620 --> 00:32:23,980
Did any particular product perform especially well this month?

709
00:32:24,570 --> 00:32:24,950
Yes.

710
00:32:25,350 --> 00:32:29,190
Our premium subscription package exceeded expectations significantly.

711
00:32:29,690 --> 00:32:30,040
Really?

712
00:32:30,080 --> 00:32:31,020
That's interesting.

713
00:32:31,420 --> 00:32:36,070
Customers responded positively after we improved customer support services last quarter.

714
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:37,000
That makes sense.

715
00:32:37,460 --> 00:32:41,370
Better service often increases customer trust and long-term loyalty.

716
00:32:41,850 --> 00:32:42,450
Exactly.

717
00:32:42,930 --> 00:32:46,990
Many customers specifically mentioned fast response times in their feedback forms.

718
00:32:47,490 --> 00:32:48,190
That's excellent.

719
00:32:48,580 --> 00:32:53,200
Customer experience has become one of the biggest competitive advantages in modern business.

720
00:32:53,740 --> 00:32:54,860
I completely agree.

721
00:32:55,360 --> 00:33:00,750
Nowadays, customers expect not only good products, but also excellent communication and support.

722
00:33:01,230 --> 00:33:01,830
Absolutely.

723
00:33:02,290 --> 00:33:05,170
However, not everything went perfectly this month.

724
00:33:05,440 --> 00:33:05,880
All right.

725
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:08,510
Let's discuss the challenges honestly, too.

726
00:33:09,010 --> 00:33:12,460
We experienced several shipping delays during the second week of the month.

727
00:33:13,180 --> 00:33:13,620
Ah, yes.

728
00:33:13,800 --> 00:33:15,340
I remember hearing about that issue.

729
00:33:15,840 --> 00:33:19,570
Unfortunately, one of our suppliers failed to deliver materials on time.

730
00:33:20,030 --> 00:33:22,980
That must have affected production schedules significantly.

731
00:33:23,190 --> 00:33:24,360
It definitely did.

732
00:33:24,900 --> 00:33:27,800
Several customer orders were delayed by three to four days.

733
00:33:28,340 --> 00:33:29,660
How did customers react?

734
00:33:30,210 --> 00:33:36,230
Some were understandably frustrated, but most appreciated the fact that we communicated openly about the delays.

735
00:33:36,780 --> 00:33:38,900
That's an important lesson for every business.

736
00:33:39,300 --> 00:33:43,430
Honest communication can reduce customer frustration even during problems.

737
00:33:43,950 --> 00:33:44,530
Exactly.

738
00:33:44,990 --> 00:33:49,130
Customers usually become more upset when companies avoid giving updates.

739
00:33:49,420 --> 00:33:50,120
Very true.

740
00:33:50,720 --> 00:33:53,180
Did the support team manage the situation well?

741
00:33:53,560 --> 00:33:53,820
Yes.

742
00:33:54,240 --> 00:33:58,030
They worked overtime, answering emails and updating customers regularly.

743
00:33:58,510 --> 00:33:59,410
I'm glad to hear that.

744
00:33:59,790 --> 00:34:02,110
They handled a difficult situation professionally.

745
00:34:02,530 --> 00:34:03,080
Definitely.

746
00:34:03,580 --> 00:34:03,960
All right.

747
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:06,360
Let's move on to the marketing department update.

748
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:07,920
Mia, would you like to begin?

749
00:34:08,420 --> 00:34:08,660
Sure.

750
00:34:08,700 --> 00:34:13,930
This month, our social media engagement improved significantly compared to previous campaigns.

751
00:34:14,430 --> 00:34:15,490
That's excellent news.

752
00:34:15,810 --> 00:34:16,470
What changed?

753
00:34:16,810 --> 00:34:22,740
We focused more on short video content and behind-the-scenes material instead of traditional advertisements.

754
00:34:23,260 --> 00:34:24,080
Interesting approach.

755
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:27,950
Why do you think customers responded better to that style of content?

756
00:34:28,370 --> 00:34:31,060
Because people enjoy authentic communication.

757
00:34:31,420 --> 00:34:35,950
They want to see real employees, real situations, and real company culture.

758
00:34:36,060 --> 00:34:37,360
That makes a lot of sense.

759
00:34:37,820 --> 00:34:42,370
Modern customers value authenticity more than perfect marketing language.

760
00:34:42,390 --> 00:34:43,070
Exactly.

761
00:34:43,490 --> 00:34:51,800
For example, one simple behind-the-scenes office video generated almost three times more engagement than our professionally designed advertisement.

762
00:34:52,240 --> 00:34:54,480
Wow, that's a huge difference.

763
00:34:54,500 --> 00:34:55,920
It really surprised us too.

764
00:34:56,480 --> 00:35:00,420
I think customers appreciate honesty and personality from brands nowadays.

765
00:35:00,980 --> 00:35:01,550
Absolutely.

766
00:35:01,970 --> 00:35:05,310
Companies feel more human when they communicate naturally online.

767
00:35:05,570 --> 00:35:09,280
Did we notice any increase in website traffic after those videos?

768
00:35:09,780 --> 00:35:10,080
Yes.

769
00:35:10,400 --> 00:35:14,250
Website visits increased by nearly 20% during the campaign period.

770
00:35:14,690 --> 00:35:15,470
That's impressive.

771
00:35:15,910 --> 00:35:19,840
We also gained many new followers across multiple social media platforms.

772
00:35:20,400 --> 00:35:21,060
Excellent work.

773
00:35:21,460 --> 00:35:24,360
Those results show the strategy is moving in the right direction.

774
00:35:24,800 --> 00:35:25,280
Thank you.

775
00:35:25,660 --> 00:35:28,270
However, we still need improvement in certain areas.

776
00:35:28,850 --> 00:35:29,310
Such as?

777
00:35:29,850 --> 00:35:33,780
Our email marketing campaign underperformed compared to expectations.

778
00:35:33,990 --> 00:35:34,390
Really?

779
00:35:34,690 --> 00:35:35,740
What do you think caused that?

780
00:35:36,140 --> 00:35:39,940
Honestly, I think the email content felt too formal and generic.

781
00:35:40,480 --> 00:35:41,570
I understand what you mean.

782
00:35:42,050 --> 00:35:45,150
Customers receive hundreds of marketing emails every week.

783
00:35:45,530 --> 00:35:48,400
Generic messages simply don't attract attention anymore.

784
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:49,940
That's very true.

785
00:35:50,440 --> 00:35:53,420
Personalized communication has become much more important.

786
00:35:53,910 --> 00:35:54,570
Exactly.

787
00:35:55,010 --> 00:35:59,610
We need to create content that feels more relevant and valuable to individual customers.

788
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:01,320
I completely agree.

789
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:04,880
We're planning to improve customer segmentation for future campaigns.

790
00:36:05,380 --> 00:36:06,860
That sounds like a smart decision.

791
00:36:07,280 --> 00:36:10,650
Better segmentation usually improves engagement significantly.

792
00:36:11,050 --> 00:36:12,030
That's what we're hoping for.

793
00:36:12,090 --> 00:36:13,340
All right, everyone.

794
00:36:13,550 --> 00:36:15,900
Let's discuss budget planning for next quarter.

795
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:19,820
Honestly, budget discussions always feel meetings more serious.

796
00:36:20,410 --> 00:36:22,210
Very true, but they're necessary.

797
00:36:22,650 --> 00:36:23,210
Definitely.

798
00:36:23,590 --> 00:36:27,660
Without proper budgeting, even strong business ideas can fail quickly.

799
00:36:27,820 --> 00:36:28,570
Exactly.

800
00:36:29,010 --> 00:36:33,250
So, according to management, we'll receive a moderate budget increase next quarter.

801
00:36:33,690 --> 00:36:34,870
That's good news, at least.

802
00:36:35,390 --> 00:36:38,820
Yes, although it's still lower than what some departments requested.

803
00:36:39,340 --> 00:36:40,140
I expected that.

804
00:36:40,540 --> 00:36:44,600
Most companies are being careful with spending right now because of economic uncertainty.

805
00:36:45,100 --> 00:36:45,540
Correct.

806
00:36:45,800 --> 00:36:52,330
That means we need to prioritize projects carefully and focus on investments with strong, measurable returns.

807
00:36:52,390 --> 00:37:18,110
completely agree for example I believe investing more in digital marketing makes sense because we can track performance clearly absolutely traditional advertising can still be useful but digital campaigns provide much better data and flexibility exactly if one online campaign performs poorly we can adjust it quickly while traditional advertisements often require much larger long-term commitments

808
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:18,640
Right.

809
00:37:18,920 --> 00:37:22,750
That flexibility is extremely valuable in modern business environments.

810
00:37:23,210 --> 00:37:23,750
Definitely.

811
00:37:24,310 --> 00:37:27,760
Does anyone have concerns regarding the proposed budget allocation?

812
00:37:28,220 --> 00:37:30,980
I'd like to mention something regarding employee training.

813
00:37:31,480 --> 00:37:31,900
Go ahead.

814
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:35,970
I think we should invest more in staff development programs next quarter.

815
00:37:36,510 --> 00:37:37,230
Interesting point.

816
00:37:37,690 --> 00:37:40,240
Why do you feel that's especially important right now?

817
00:37:40,450 --> 00:37:48,680
Because technology and business trends are changing rapidly, employees need continuous learning opportunities to remain competitive and productive.

818
00:37:49,300 --> 00:37:50,610
I completely agree with that.

819
00:37:51,050 --> 00:37:55,530
Plus, professional development improves employee motivation and job satisfaction.

820
00:37:56,050 --> 00:37:56,590
Very true.

821
00:37:56,630 --> 00:38:00,660
Employees usually perform better when companies invest in their growth.

822
00:38:01,160 --> 00:38:01,840
Exactly.

823
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:04,940
It also helps with long-term employee retention.

824
00:38:05,160 --> 00:38:06,440
That's an excellent point.

825
00:38:06,900 --> 00:38:10,710
Replacing experienced employees can become extremely expensive.

826
00:38:11,130 --> 00:38:11,690
Definitely.

827
00:38:12,090 --> 00:38:15,430
Recruitment and training require significant time and resources.

828
00:38:16,010 --> 00:38:21,320
I think management would support additional training investments if we present clear long-term benefits.

829
00:38:21,800 --> 00:38:23,670
I can prepare a proposal for that if needed.

830
00:38:24,170 --> 00:38:25,370
That would be very helpful.

831
00:38:25,570 --> 00:38:25,910
Thank you.

832
00:38:26,330 --> 00:38:26,850
No problem.

833
00:38:27,310 --> 00:38:27,690
All right.

834
00:38:27,910 --> 00:38:30,920
Let's discuss internal communication improvements next.

835
00:38:31,380 --> 00:38:34,960
Honestly, I think this is one of the most important topics today.

836
00:38:35,090 --> 00:38:35,660
I agree.

837
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:39,080
Good communication affects every department in the company.

838
00:38:39,520 --> 00:38:40,160
Exactly.

839
00:38:40,620 --> 00:38:45,510
Recently, several small misunderstandings created unnecessary delays between teams.

840
00:38:46,130 --> 00:38:47,070
I noticed that too.

841
00:38:47,550 --> 00:38:54,680
For example, last week the sales team launched a promotion before customer support received complete information about the campaign.

842
00:38:55,180 --> 00:38:55,800
Exactly.

843
00:38:56,140 --> 00:38:59,380
That created frustration for both employees and customers.

844
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:03,030
So, how can we improve communication moving forward?

845
00:39:03,240 --> 00:39:07,430
I think shorter weekly cross-department update meetings could help significantly.

846
00:39:07,930 --> 00:39:09,190
That's a practical idea.

847
00:39:09,750 --> 00:39:15,020
Even 15-minute meetings could help teams stay informed about major projects and updates.

848
00:39:15,040 --> 00:39:15,480
I agree.

849
00:39:15,940 --> 00:39:20,150
Small communication improvements often prevent large operational problems later.

850
00:39:20,630 --> 00:39:21,190
Exactly.

851
00:39:21,650 --> 00:39:25,910
I also think project management tools could improve transparency between departments.

852
00:39:26,420 --> 00:39:26,940
Definitely.

853
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:31,660
Shared systems help everyone track progress and responsibilities more clearly.

854
00:39:31,860 --> 00:39:36,090
Right now, too much information stays isolated within individual teams.

855
00:39:36,670 --> 00:39:40,130
And that creates confusion when projects become more complex.

856
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:41,280
Exactly.

857
00:39:41,780 --> 00:39:45,420
Better communication also improves workplace relationships overall.

858
00:39:45,940 --> 00:39:46,500
Very true.

859
00:39:46,960 --> 00:39:51,070
Teams collaborate more effectively when communication feels open and respectful.

860
00:39:51,570 --> 00:39:52,150
Absolutely.

861
00:39:52,170 --> 00:39:53,510
All right, everyone.

862
00:39:53,710 --> 00:39:58,040
Before we finish, let's quickly review the key decisions from today's meeting.

863
00:39:58,140 --> 00:39:58,760
Good idea.

864
00:39:59,180 --> 00:40:02,200
Summaries help everyone stay aligned after long discussions.

865
00:40:02,740 --> 00:40:08,550
First, we'll continue focusing heavily on short form video marketing because results have been very positive.

866
00:40:09,070 --> 00:40:10,070
Understood.

867
00:40:10,090 --> 00:40:15,510
Second, the marketing team will improve email personalization and customer segmentation strategies.

868
00:40:16,010 --> 00:40:16,340
Got it.

869
00:40:16,800 --> 00:40:22,500
Third, management will review increased investment in employee training and professional development programs.

870
00:40:22,980 --> 00:40:23,720
Sounds good.

871
00:40:23,790 --> 00:40:29,700
Fourth, departments will begin participating in short weekly communication meetings to improve coordination.

872
00:40:30,200 --> 00:40:32,280
I think that will help teamwork significantly.

873
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:33,220
I agree.

874
00:40:33,260 --> 00:40:36,590
Honestly, today's meeting felt very productive overall.

875
00:40:37,090 --> 00:40:37,790
I think so too.

876
00:40:38,350 --> 00:40:41,900
Everyone shared useful ideas and constructive feedback professionally.

877
00:40:42,360 --> 00:40:44,300
That's what makes business meetings valuable.

878
00:40:44,660 --> 00:40:47,990
Collaboration, problem solving, and clear communication.

879
00:40:48,070 --> 00:40:48,790
Exactly.

880
00:40:49,230 --> 00:40:53,440
Meetings should help teams move forward together instead of simply wasting time.

881
00:40:53,860 --> 00:40:54,500
Very true.

882
00:40:54,980 --> 00:40:58,580
Does anyone have final comments or questions before we end today's meeting?

883
00:40:59,000 --> 00:40:59,610
Nothing for me.

884
00:41:00,130 --> 00:41:00,590
Same here.

885
00:41:01,010 --> 00:41:01,470
All right then.

886
00:41:01,770 --> 00:41:04,130
Thank you everyone for your hard work and participation.

887
00:41:04,150 --> 00:41:12,760
Hello everyone and welcome back to the English at Work podcast where we help you understand real workplace English through practical, natural conversations.

888
00:41:13,080 --> 00:41:13,700
Hi, everyone.

889
00:41:13,740 --> 00:41:15,180
Thanks for joining us again.

890
00:41:15,680 --> 00:41:17,740
Today's episode is a special one.

891
00:41:18,180 --> 00:41:22,150
We're focusing on business meeting conversations in 60 minutes.

892
00:41:22,710 --> 00:41:28,640
This is a full office simulation of how meetings can happen over an hour in real business environments.

893
00:41:29,240 --> 00:41:29,780
Exactly.

894
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:41,230
We'll take you step by step through a full meeting cycle, starting from preparation, opening the meeting, discussions, disagreements, decision making, and finally closing and follow up.

895
00:41:41,380 --> 00:41:49,990
And, as always, we will also explain important phrases so you understand not just what people say, but why they say it in that way.

896
00:41:50,570 --> 00:41:52,790
All right, let's begin the meeting simulation.

897
00:41:53,310 --> 00:41:54,110
Good morning, everyone.

898
00:41:54,330 --> 00:41:55,090
Let's get started.

899
00:41:55,470 --> 00:41:58,500
Thank you for joining today's 60-minute project review meeting.

900
00:41:58,980 --> 00:42:03,160
Before we begin, I'd like to quickly go through the agenda so we are all aligned.

901
00:42:03,640 --> 00:42:10,390
Today, we will cover project progress, budget updates, client feedback, risk analysis, and action planning.

902
00:42:10,620 --> 00:42:13,930
When someone says, so we are all aligned, what does that mean?

903
00:42:13,990 --> 00:42:17,110
It means everyone has the same understanding of the plan.

904
00:42:17,590 --> 00:42:21,260
In meetings, alignment is important so there is no confusion later.

905
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:22,000
Great.

906
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:24,380
Let's begin with project progress.

907
00:42:24,980 --> 00:42:27,010
Sarah, could you start with the development update?

908
00:42:27,470 --> 00:42:27,690
Sure.

909
00:42:27,730 --> 00:42:31,350
As of today, we are 70% complete with development.

910
00:42:31,770 --> 00:42:38,300
The back-end system is stable, but we are still working on improving performance in the login and dashboard sections.

911
00:42:38,520 --> 00:42:44,030
When you say still working on, it means the task is not finished yet, but progress is ongoing, right?

912
00:42:44,590 --> 00:42:45,050
Exactly.

913
00:42:45,330 --> 00:42:46,730
It shows continuous effort.

914
00:42:47,150 --> 00:42:48,090
Are there any blockers?

915
00:42:48,650 --> 00:42:48,930
Yes.

916
00:42:49,250 --> 00:42:54,480
We have a dependency issue with the API integration team, which is slightly delaying testing.

917
00:42:54,940 --> 00:42:57,320
Blocker is a very important business term.

918
00:42:57,720 --> 00:43:01,070
It means something that stops progress completely or slows it down.

919
00:43:01,610 --> 00:43:01,810
Right.

920
00:43:02,210 --> 00:43:04,150
Now let's move to budget updates.

921
00:43:04,300 --> 00:43:09,470
The current budget is within limits, but we are close to the maximum allocation for development resources.

922
00:43:10,010 --> 00:43:12,090
When you say allocation, what does it mean?

923
00:43:12,130 --> 00:43:17,240
Allocation means how money, time, or resources are distributed across tasks.

924
00:43:17,700 --> 00:43:18,120
Got it.

925
00:43:18,360 --> 00:43:21,290
So we may need to monitor spending more carefully.

926
00:43:21,750 --> 00:43:22,750
Yes, exactly.

927
00:43:23,290 --> 00:43:25,730
Now let's move to client feedback discussion.

928
00:43:26,170 --> 00:43:32,180
The client has given mostly positive feedback, but they raised concerns about user experience on mobile devices.

929
00:43:32,420 --> 00:43:37,160
When you say raised concerns, it means they pointed out problems in a polite way.

930
00:43:37,700 --> 00:43:40,610
Yes, it is a professional way of saying they noticed issues.

931
00:43:41,170 --> 00:43:42,070
What is our response?

932
00:43:42,570 --> 00:43:45,550
We're prioritizing mobile optimization in the next sprint.

933
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:49,200
Sprint here refers to a short development cycle, right?

934
00:43:49,620 --> 00:43:50,200
Exactly.

935
00:43:50,460 --> 00:43:52,400
It is commonly used in project management.

936
00:43:52,920 --> 00:43:55,190
Now, let's move to risk analysis.

937
00:43:55,830 --> 00:43:59,350
One potential risk is delay in third-party API delivery.

938
00:43:59,640 --> 00:44:05,500
When we say potential risk, it means something that may cause problems in the future but has not happened yet.

939
00:44:06,040 --> 00:44:07,750
So how we manage this risk?

940
00:44:08,170 --> 00:44:11,810
We're preparing a backup API solution in case delays continue.

941
00:44:12,230 --> 00:44:13,230
That's a smart approach.

942
00:44:13,780 --> 00:44:16,460
Now let's move to discussion and opinions.

943
00:44:16,480 --> 00:44:20,700
I'd like to open the floor for suggestions regarding performance improvement.

944
00:44:20,720 --> 00:44:24,070
Open the floor means inviting everyone to share ideas in a meeting.

945
00:44:24,570 --> 00:44:24,850
Yes.

946
00:44:25,350 --> 00:44:26,770
Does anyone have input?

947
00:44:26,940 --> 00:44:30,880
I think we should reduce unnecessary background processes to improve speed.

948
00:44:31,410 --> 00:44:32,350
That's a valid point.

949
00:44:32,670 --> 00:44:33,970
Thank you for your input.

950
00:44:33,990 --> 00:44:37,810
I agree, but I also think we should optimize database queries first.

951
00:44:38,350 --> 00:44:43,980
When someone says, I agree, but it shows partial agreement followed by a different opinion.

952
00:44:44,440 --> 00:44:44,980
Exactly.

953
00:44:45,180 --> 00:44:46,660
It is a polite way to disagree.

954
00:44:47,140 --> 00:44:48,190
Any other suggestions?

955
00:44:48,630 --> 00:44:51,810
We could also improve caching strategies to reduce loading time.

956
00:44:52,230 --> 00:44:52,690
Good idea.

957
00:44:53,030 --> 00:44:53,850
Let's note that down.

958
00:44:54,350 --> 00:44:56,460
Now, let's move to disagreement handling.

959
00:44:56,740 --> 00:45:01,130
I understand your point, but I think we should delay the launch by one week.

960
00:45:01,650 --> 00:45:02,610
Why do you suggest that?

961
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:08,660
Because if we launch too early, we may face performance issues that affect customer satisfaction.

962
00:45:09,230 --> 00:45:10,230
I see your concern.

963
00:45:10,330 --> 00:45:13,280
However, delaying the launch might impact marketing plans.

964
00:45:13,780 --> 00:45:14,540
That's true.

965
00:45:14,600 --> 00:45:16,940
This is why we need to find a balance.

966
00:45:17,040 --> 00:45:19,520
Maybe we can launch a soft version first.

967
00:45:20,180 --> 00:45:24,470
Soft launch means releasing a limited version to test performance, right?

968
00:45:24,930 --> 00:45:25,390
Exactly.

969
00:45:25,950 --> 00:45:27,390
I think that's a good compromise.

970
00:45:27,890 --> 00:45:29,750
Now, let's move to decision making.

971
00:45:30,250 --> 00:45:33,380
Based on the discussion, we have three main decisions.

972
00:45:33,940 --> 00:45:36,860
First, we will optimize mobile performance immediately.

973
00:45:37,360 --> 00:45:40,240
Second, we will prepare a backup API system.

974
00:45:40,780 --> 00:45:43,710
Third, we will proceed with a soft launch strategy.

975
00:45:43,960 --> 00:45:48,780
When someone says, based on the discussion, it shows a summary of what has been agreed.

976
00:45:49,240 --> 00:45:51,360
Yes, it helps formalize decisions.

977
00:45:51,930 --> 00:45:54,150
Now, let's talk about responsibilities.

978
00:45:54,630 --> 00:45:56,490
Who will handle performance optimization?

979
00:45:56,950 --> 00:45:59,250
The development team will take responsibility for that.

980
00:45:59,810 --> 00:46:01,740
And who will manage client communication?

981
00:46:02,200 --> 00:46:04,660
The project manager will handle all client updates.

982
00:46:05,200 --> 00:46:05,480
Good.

983
00:46:05,940 --> 00:46:08,740
Assigning responsibilities is very important in meetings.

984
00:46:09,260 --> 00:46:12,220
Now, let's move to time management and deadlines.

985
00:46:12,390 --> 00:46:15,390
We are currently working with a tight deadline for next Friday.

986
00:46:15,920 --> 00:46:19,400
Tight deadline means there's very limited time to complete the work.

987
00:46:19,880 --> 00:46:23,330
Yes, so we need to prioritize critical tasks.

988
00:46:23,350 --> 00:46:25,050
What happens if we miss the deadline?

989
00:46:25,450 --> 00:46:28,610
It could delay the entire product launch and affect client trust.

990
00:46:29,070 --> 00:46:29,890
That's serious.

991
00:46:30,440 --> 00:46:32,540
Now let's move to problem-solving discussion.

992
00:46:33,120 --> 00:46:35,760
We are facing a performance bottleneck in the system.

993
00:46:36,280 --> 00:46:39,430
Bottleneck means a point that slows down the entire process.

994
00:46:39,970 --> 00:46:40,450
Exactly.

995
00:46:40,910 --> 00:46:41,890
What is the solution?

996
00:46:42,060 --> 00:46:45,400
We can scale server capacity and optimize database queries.

997
00:46:45,880 --> 00:46:46,380
Good approach.

998
00:46:46,900 --> 00:46:49,000
Now, let's move to communication updates.

999
00:46:49,560 --> 00:46:53,070
I will send a follow-up email after this meeting summarizing everything.

1000
00:46:53,590 --> 00:46:58,210
Follow-up email means a message sent after the meeting to confirm decisions and actions.

1001
00:46:58,710 --> 00:47:00,710
Yes, it helps everyone stay informed.

1002
00:47:01,230 --> 00:47:03,240
Now, let's move to final questions.

1003
00:47:03,700 --> 00:47:06,220
Does anyone have any concerns before we close?

1004
00:47:06,660 --> 00:47:08,660
I just want to confirm the next meeting schedule.

1005
00:47:09,140 --> 00:47:11,140
It will be next Wednesday at 10 a.m.

1006
00:47:11,560 --> 00:47:12,060
Perfect.

1007
00:47:12,300 --> 00:47:14,040
Alright, let's wrap this up.

1008
00:47:14,500 --> 00:47:17,930
Let's wrap this up means let's finish the meeting in a structured way.

1009
00:47:18,450 --> 00:47:20,810
Thank you everyone for your time and contributions.

1010
00:47:21,310 --> 00:47:24,040
And now, let's simulate a post-meeting discussion.

1011
00:47:24,540 --> 00:47:26,820
That was a long meeting, but very productive.

1012
00:47:27,320 --> 00:47:31,030
Yes, we covered everything from progress to risks to decisions.

1013
00:47:31,610 --> 00:47:34,550
I think the soft launch idea is the most practical solution.

1014
00:47:35,090 --> 00:47:35,510
I agree.

1015
00:47:35,780 --> 00:47:37,320
It balances speed and quality.

1016
00:47:37,800 --> 00:47:39,620
We should document everything clearly.

1017
00:47:39,720 --> 00:47:42,820
Yes, documentation is important, so nothing is missed.

1018
00:47:43,340 --> 00:47:46,630
Now, let's imagine a short manager reflection after the meeting.

1019
00:47:47,170 --> 00:47:49,350
Overall, the team handled the discussion well.

1020
00:47:49,710 --> 00:47:52,070
There was good participation and clear communication.

1021
00:47:52,740 --> 00:47:54,900
One improvement area is time management.

1022
00:47:55,360 --> 00:47:57,680
Some discussions were slightly longer than needed.

1023
00:47:58,100 --> 00:47:59,820
But overall, the meeting was effective.

1024
00:48:00,300 --> 00:48:03,670
Now, let's summarize key meeting phrases we used today.

1025
00:48:03,800 --> 00:48:16,300
We used phrases like we are aligned, blocker, allocation, raise concerns, open the floor, input, I see your point but, based on the discussion, and let's wrap this up.

1026
00:48:16,780 --> 00:48:19,700
These are essential phrases for real business meetings.

1027
00:48:20,220 --> 00:48:21,680
And the key idea is simple.

1028
00:48:22,260 --> 00:48:24,910
English is about clarity, structure, and politeness.

1029
00:48:25,470 --> 00:48:27,090
You don't need complex words.

1030
00:48:27,410 --> 00:48:29,980
You need clear communication and confidence.

1031
00:48:30,090 --> 00:48:33,940
And that brings us to the end of our 60-minute business meeting simulation.

1032
00:48:34,360 --> 00:48:37,440
Thanks for staying with us through this full office experience.

1033
00:48:37,910 --> 00:48:42,210
Keep practicing these phrases, and soon you will feel confident in any business meeting.

1034
00:48:42,550 --> 00:48:58,980
Goodbye, everyone, and see you in the next episode.
